---
/* THE STORY (25 October 2026). The direct line told once as a short book: one chapter per generation, from
   Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen at the top of the spine to Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen, a chapter on the family's
   own war testimony tested claim by claim, and a short closing chapter for the living. David asked for one easy
   first place to go, and this is it: the first chapter after the cover in book.json, a card on the home page and
   the first item in the Start menu.

   EVERYTHING ON THE PAGE IS DATA. The words, the marks, the trees and the maps are src/data/story.json; the page,
   ribbon, trees and maps are the kit's (StoryBook, kit/docs/story.md). `npm run check:story` (the kit's
   checkfamilystory.py) holds the data to its grammar and reads the built page back: a grade on every paragraph,
   a source on every passage about the world, a link under every chapter, the living with no dates.

   NO PLATES. Every scan in this archive is MyHeritage-sourced or of no recorded provenance, and David has not ruled
   on MyHeritage's terms, so the linking standard keeps every scan link-only (standard.json scans.link_only). The
   chapters quote the documents in words and link to the transcriptions on /documents/. */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StoryBook from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/StoryBook.astro";
import book from "../data/story.json";
/* THE MINI FLAGS (27 October 2026): each card's flags are in src/data/ribbon-flags.json (tools/ribbonflags.py, the kit's generator); storyWithFlags/familyWithFlags put them on the cards. */
import { storyWithFlags } from "../lib/ribbonflags.js";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
/* Generations five and six (the ribbon's two and three) are both Petrus Jacobus Booysen and share one person page,
   which holds both men (dossiers.json `also`). Since 27 October 2026 the ribbon's card for the son names no person:
   direct-line.json (made from this ribbon by the kit's directline.py) lists each person once, and a card that pointed
   at his father's page would tint and band that page as two generations. He is on the line all the same (the file's
   `unlinked`, tinted as gen:3), and the prose and trees still link him to the shared page and say which man is meant.
   THE TINT: `directLine` tints every ribbon card and tree node of someone on our direct line (kit/docs/family-ribbon.md). */
import directLine from "../data/direct-line.json";
const personHref = (slug) => u(`/people/${slug}/`);
---
<Base title={book.title} eyebrow={book.eyebrow} dek={book.dek}>
  <StoryBook book={storyWithFlags(book)} personHref={personHref} u={u} directLine={directLine} />
</Base>
